Here are the limits of each function at $x=0$, according to the graphs:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}h(x)=4}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}g(x)=0}
\end{align}$

Now we can find $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{h(x)}{g(x)}$ using the fact that the limit of a quotient of functions is the quotient of the individual limits (as long as the limit in the denominator isn't equal to $0$):

$\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{h(x)}{g(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}h(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}g(x)}$

Putting it all together:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{h(x)}{g(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}h(x)}}{\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}g(x)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD 4}{\maroonD 0}
\end{align}$

We ended up with a nonzero number divided by zero, so the limit doesn't exist.
